We could copy the contract into the chat ourselves. To make the investigation more independent, we can instead expose a file-reading tool. The model receives a description of what the tool does and the arguments it accepts. It can then return a request such as this schematic payload:

```json
{
  "tool": "read_file",
  "arguments": { "path": "src/retry.js" }
}
```

The exact message format depends on the tool interface. What matters is the handoff. The model has selected an operation and supplied its arguments. A program outside the model now has to interpret that request, decide whether it is allowed, execute it and report the result.

Suppose the read succeeds. The program sends the file contents back into the conversation, identified as the result of that read. A subsequent model call can compare the actual loop with the contract. If the request is denied, or the path does not exist, that result is also information. It changes which next actions make sense.

There are therefore three distinct events: requesting a read, reading the file, and making the read result available to the next call. A system can fail between any pair of them. It might display a tool request without dispatching it. It might read the right bytes and then accidentally omit them from the next input. A transcript saying “I inspected the source” is weaker evidence than a recorded tool result containing the source that was actually inspected.
